<resources>
    <!--
      PB-DS-2 and PB-DS-3 (S22b): the typography scale.

      17 product text styles across 6 sizes: PB-DS-2's 18 across 12 sizes, 4 weights and 5
      tracking values, plus the one docs/design/substrate-components.md §7 adds - `Display.SAS`,
      the 34 sp the pairing screen's verification emoji are set in - less the two ADR-012 takes
      out, and spent across the FIVE RUNGS ADR-012 phase 2 rules plus the SAS's own 34.

      THE HEADING COUNT WAS 18/12, THEN 19/13, THEN 17/12, AND IS 17/6 SINCE 2026-08-09 (ADR-012,
      beads agents-tracker-ksvb.8 and agents-tracker-nx44.9). The requirement's numbers are
      unchanged and are named as the requirement's; the file's own count leads, because that is
      what a reader of this file is counting. What moved: `Label.CardHead` merged into `Mono.Meta`
      (T1) and `Label.StatusBar` was deleted (T2), which also took 13 sp out of the ladder - it
      was that size's only occupant. Both are recorded in android/gate/s22b_type_test.go's
      s22bUnimplementedRules, which is where a rule the design draws and this app does not spend
      has to name the decision that says so. NO PIXEL MOVED IN PHASE 1: `.tcard .h` and
      `.sheet2 .ctx` render identically once ADR-009 D7's two-face bundle resolves 600 to 500, and
      `.ptime` is the mock's status-bar clock, which this app has never drawn. ADR-009 D7's own
      "(19 styles, ...)" is the record of what D7 did not change and carries a dated pointer here.

      THEN PHASE 2 MOVED TWELVE PIXELS' WORTH OF SIZE, ON THE OWNER'S RULING, AND NO STYLE LEFT.
      The field test's "fonts not well mastered" was, structurally, ten sizes inside a six-point
      band with every half-step occupied. Ruling R1 (2026-08-09) consolidates them onto five rungs
      - 10 micro, 11.5 code, 12.5 body, 14 title, 22 display - and R3 takes the display rung to the
      signed maquette's 22. Every style keeps its name and its citation, because the design still
      distinguishes the roles; what they share now is a size.

      Before this file the app expressed zero of them: 1582 lines of surface code held one
      Typeface.MONOSPACE, one Typeface.BOLD, and no setTextSize or setLetterSpacing anywhere.

      EACH STYLE DECLARES THE DESIGN FACT IT DESCENDS FROM, in the annotation above it. That
      comment is machine-read - android/gate/s22b_type_test.go and the Robolectric suite both
      parse it - and it is the only thing in this file that is a decision. Every NUMBER below is
      read out of that fact at test time and compared, in both directions: a style whose citation
      names nothing fails, and a design fact with no style here fails too - unless not
      implementing it is ITSELF a recorded decision, which since ADR-012 two rules are, and the
      gate holds each of those to the ADR that decided it rather than to a list it keeps quietly.
      So re-pointing a style at a different rule is a visible claim.

      SINCE 2026-08-09 THE SIZE IS THE ONE NUMBER THAT COMES FROM SOMEWHERE ELSE, and it is not
      this file either. A rung is a decision about THIS APP's hierarchy and no CSS rule can state
      one, so ADR-012 phase 2's rung table is where a style's sp lives; both readers parse that
      table, key it by style name, and fail on a size here that disagrees with it. The table also
      carries each rule's own px and is asserted against the artifact, so a design that moves a
      rule leaves the record visibly stale instead of quietly authorizing a size nobody compared.
      A size still cannot be edited here without a record disagreeing; what changed is which
      record.

      THERE ARE TWO CLASSES OF CITATION, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS WHERE THE AUTHORITY LIVES.
      `origin:` names a CSS rule in the shared block of
      docs/research/remote-control-design-directions.html - sixteen of the seventeen styles are
      that, and they are transcriptions of a rule the artifact draws. `derived:` names a row of
      docs/design/substrate-components.md, which is where the design decided something the
      artifact never drew. `Display.SAS` is the only one: the artifact has no `.sas` rule at all,
      because it renders four candidate skins and not the pairing flow, so citing `origin: .sas`
      would claim a join to a rule that does not exist. The kit already made this distinction for
      the same reason - EmptyState.kt cites `derived:` because `.empty` is the mock's class - and
      the gate counts the two classes separately so neither can absorb a style belonging to the
      other.

      NO STYLE CARRIES A COLOUR, and that is deliberate. PB-DS-2's sentence lists colour among the
      attributes; the same style renders in several colours - Label.Button is the hero ink on the
      approve variant, the error red on the deny variant and the primary ink on the tertiary one,
      from ONE CSS rule - so a TextAppearance that carried one would be wrong at two of its three
      call sites. Colour is applied by the component kit (PB-DS-6, S23), and the gate asserts the
      absence so it stays a decision rather than becoming a habit.

      SIZES ARE sp, NOT dp. The design is a fixed 386x812 frame and dp would reproduce it exactly,
      which is the argument for dp and it is the wrong one: dp text does not respond to the user's
      font-size setting at all. PB-DS-12 makes surviving a 1.3x scale a floor, and a scale that
      shipped in dp would have decided against it here, silently, for every screen.

      LINE HEIGHT IS AN ABSOLUTE DIMENSION, because Android has no unitless multiplier on a
      TextAppearance. Each value below is the design's multiplier times THE SIZE THE STYLE RENDERS
      AT, which since ADR-012 phase 2 is the rung and not the CSS px - three of the five leadings
      moved with their rung and nobody typed one - and the gate recomputes the product rather than
      trusting it. It appears only on the five styles whose
      CSS rule declares a line-height; the other twelve inherit the platform's, which is what the
      design does. `.prow .ln b` is the interesting omission: it inherits 1.4 from `.prow .ln` in
      CSS, and on Android it is an inline span inside somebody else's paragraph, where line height
      is the paragraph's property and not the span's. There is nothing for it to carry.

      FAMILIES ARE SUBSTITUTIONS (ADR-007 B134 decision 2, as amended by ADR-009 D7). The design
      names SF Pro and SF Mono; neither is licensable off Apple, so every style in this app renders
      a substitute, and the point of the decision is that the substitute is chosen once and written
      down. The SANS half is unchanged: `sans-serif`, no bundled asset, and every weight here is
      reachable because android:textFontWeight resolves against the platform's variable Roboto at
      API 33, which minSdk guarantees.

      THE MONO HALF IS `@font/jetbrains_mono` FROM ADR-009 D7 (phase O5), and the paragraph below
      is why. The bundled family answers 400 and 500; a mono style asking for 600 resolves to the
      nearest, which is 500, with no synthetic bolding, and both faces advance 600/1000 em on every
      glyph so the frame's grid does not depend on which weight asked for it.

      THE MONO STYLES CARRY `tnum, zero, calt` AND THE SANS STYLES CARRY NOTHING, which is ADR-009
      D7's "wherever machine data renders" made mechanical: every rule the design sets in
      `var(p-mono)` is a machine's own register, and nothing proportional in this app renders a
      number the machine produced. The attribute is a full OVERRIDE rather than an addition, so
      `calt` is stated even though the family ships it on: naming two features without it would
      switch the contextual alternates off. The gate asserts both directions.

      SINCE 2026-08-09 ONE STYLE'S FAMILY IS A RULING RATHER THAN A CITATION (ADR-012 phase 2 P9,
      owner ruling R2). `Label.Section` cites `.plabel`, which is mono in the design source; the
      ruling restates ADR-009 D7's "wherever machine data renders" as the standing boundary —
      mono is for data the MACHINE produced (agent names, code, ids, timestamps) and nothing else,
      so a section header the design WROTE is the app speaking and takes sans-serif, at the
      ruling's own specimen tracking (0.11em) rather than `.plabel`'s 0.09em. The gate reads this
      one exception from ADR-012 phase 2 P9 rather than from the citation, the same shape the rung
      table already takes for size.

      ADR-009 (O2) MOVED FOUR STYLES AND NOBODY TYPED A NUMBER TO DO IT. The two display styles
      lost 150 units of weight (650 to 500) and the four tracking values that cite a token
      changed sign (body tracking goes POSITIVE on light-on-dark text, per the reading-ergonomics
      guidance the ADR cites). Both facts arrived the only way this file accepts a number: the
      CSS rules those styles cite state their weight and tracking as `var(p-display-wt)` and
      `var(p-body-tr)` (leading dashes elided, as everywhere else in this file), the gate
      resolves those vars through internal/design/tokens.json, and this file had to follow or
      fail. The styles whose CSS states a literal weight, 600 on a row title and 400 on a
      message, did not move, because the design did not move them. That is the difference
      between a skin change and a find-and-replace.

      THE RECORDED RESIDUAL IS DEAD, AND THIS IS WHAT IT WAS. B134 predicted that `monospace` may
      show TOFU for U+2500-257F. Measured (MonoBoxDrawingTest), the box-drawing glyphs all resolved
      instead, through font FALLBACK, at 0.71em against the mono family's own 0.60em: the terminal
      peek's frame rendered 18 percent wider per character than the text it framed and did not
      align. That is a worse failure than tofu, because tofu is visible to anyone who looks at the
      screen once. ADR-009 D7 bundles JetBrains Mono, which covers the block at the family's own
      advance, and the same test now asserts the equality instead of the mismatch. It keeps the old
      inequality against Typeface.MONOSPACE, so the defect is still shown to be real one family
      over rather than assumed away.

      Parents are stated explicitly on every style. `A.B.C` inherits from `A.B` implicitly in
      aapt, and the intermediate names here (TextAppearance.Swarm.Display, .Title, .Label, .Body,
      .Mono) are groupings rather than styles - none of them exists, and an implicit parent that
      does not exist is a build error.
    -->

    <!--
      derived: docs/design/substrate-components.md §7 Display.SAS

      The SAS display: the four verification emoji a person compares against the ones their Mac
      shows. §7 states 34 sp / 400 / sans and nothing else, so this style states nothing else
      either - tracking is CSS's `normal`, which is 0, and there is no leading, because a row that
      does not give one is not a row somebody can read a leading out of.

      IT CARRIES NO INK, and for a stronger reason than the rest of this file's. Derivation row 7
      records it as an exception: emoji glyphs are drawn by the platform's colour emoji font, which
      ignores textColor entirely. There is no token that could apply.
    -->
    <style name="TextAppearance.Swarm.Display.SAS" parent="TextAppearance.AppCompat">
        <item name="android:textSize">34sp</item>
        <item name="android:textFontWeight">400</item>
        <item name="android:letterSpacing">0</item>
        <item name="android:fontFamily">sans-serif</item>
    </style>

    <!-- origin: .pnav .big -->
    <style name="TextAppearance.Swarm.Display.NavTitle" parent="TextAppearance.AppCompat">
        <item name="android:textSize">22sp</item>
        <item name="android:textFontWeight">500</item>
        <item name="android:letterSpacing">-0.015</item>
        <item name="android:fontFamily">sans-serif</item>
    </style>

